  "answer_text": "Great question! Making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also called strabismus or amblyopia) can feel awkward at first, but it gets easier with a few simple tips. Here's how to handle it gracefully:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Look at the Eye That's Looking at You**\nLook at the eye that is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be rude, it may feel to them as though you are staring at their affected eye.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Remember \u2014 They ARE Making Eye Contact With You**\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one eye is looking at you and the other is looking somewhere else, which can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact. Keep in mind that both their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one just has a hard time staying centered. For most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you, so don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Try a Soft or Blurred Focus**\nIf there's something on someone's face you don't want to stare at rudely, blurring your eyes lets you look straight at them and listen better. This can help you avoid fixating on either eye individually.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Don't Stare or Switch Eyes Repeatedly**\nGive yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while. It won't detract from the conversation if you naturally shift your focus from eye to eye, as we all do during normal eye contact.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **If You Know Them Well, You Can Acknowledge It**\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. A lighthearted comment like \"Sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\" can lighten a possibly awkward situation. However, don't use this approach with a stranger or someone you've just met \u2014 just move on. When you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Relax and Be Natural**\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them, and other things about that person start to form your dominant image. The more you interact with them, the more natural it will feel!",
